---
/**
 * THE HORIZON. One canvas, one composition, in our green.
 *
 * WHERE THE ART DIRECTION COMES FROM. `packages/corpus/data/ui-references/web-landing.json`,
 * reference `rosebud-ai-design-language`, third measured claim: "The entire atmosphere is ONE fixed
 * image: a div at position:fixed, 100lvw by 100lvh, z-index -1, background-image:
 * front-page-background.webp. No gradient stack, no layers, no parallax." That image is a synthwave
 * horizon — a perspective grid receding to a glowing band. Rule 17 says the same thing as an
 * instruction: "Buy the atmosphere with one piece of art direction, not with a stack of layers,"
 * against our own five-mechanism atmosphere that the owner still called static.
 *
 * WE CANNOT USE THEIRS. The fourth caution on that reference is explicit: "the background image in
 * particular is THEIR art and must not be fetched or reproduced — ours has to be our own." So this
 * file is that composition BUILT, in the accent this page already owns, from four numbers that are
 * ours: a vanishing point, a floor, a glow band and a sky.
 *
 * AND WHY IT MOVES, when the reference measurably does not. The same reference's second claim reads
 * "ZERO @keyframes. ZERO running animations. ZERO <canvas>." The owner was shown that measurement
 * and asked for the opposite anyway — "you have to build the most keyframes, running animations,
 * canvas ever". That is not a contradiction to resolve by splitting the difference. It is the
 * brief: take their surfaces, radii, accent family, control heights and transition bands, and put a
 * serious ambient motion layer under them. The transition bands (rule 16, nothing over 0.26s) bind
 * INTERACTION. Ambient motion is exempt, and this is ambient.
 *
 * ================================ WHAT IS DRAWN, AND WHY IT IS DRAWN IN THAT ORDER
 *
 *   1. THE GLOW BAND, a vertical gradient in --accent peaking exactly on the horizon line and
 *      falling off both ways — 120px up into the sky, 200px down onto the floor — plus a 1.2px
 *      core so the band has an edge rather than being a smudge. Rule 13: "Elevate with a glow, and
 *      keep the one real shadow inset." The whole page's elevation vocabulary is a glow; this is
 *      that vocabulary at the scale of the viewport.
 *
 *   2. THE FLOOR. Rails are equally spaced along the BOTTOM EDGE and every one of them is drawn to
 *      the vanishing point. That is not an approximation of perspective, it is perspective: lines
 *      parallel to the view direction meet at the vanishing point, and their crossings of any one
 *      horizontal scanline are equally spaced. Rungs are horizontal, at y = vpy + floor/(n - phase)
 *      for n = 1..26, which is the projection of a ground plane's evenly spaced cross-lines. `phase`
 *      runs 0 -> 1 and wraps, and because the wrap just renames line n as line n-1, the scroll is
 *      seamless and has no keyframe boundary to see.
 *
 *   3. THE BOTTOM WASH, in --ground. Without it the near rungs terminate against the edge of the
 *      viewport in a hard bright bar. With it the floor arrives out of the page's own colour.
 *
 *   4. THE SKY. Ninety particles, above the horizon only, lit by the band — alpha strongest near
 *      it, falling to a quarter at the top of the frame — drifting up and across, and carrying a
 *      little parallax off the vanishing point so the sky leans when the floor does.
 *
 * ================================ THE DECORATION YIELDS, AND HERE IS THE ARITHMETIC
 *
 * `apps/web/tests/decoration-yields-to-the-composer.test.mjs` records the rule the hard way: a mesh
 * that was densest directly behind the composer read, correctly, as "broken". The horizon line here
 * sits at 0.74 of the viewport height, so EVERY grid line — the densest thing this file draws — is
 * in the bottom 26% of the frame. A hero whose content is centred puts its composer around 0.51h to
 * 0.65h. The only thing this file paints in that band is the upper tail of the glow and a scattering
 * of 1px dots, which is the thinnest part of the composition by construction rather than by luck.
 *
 * ================================ THE THREE WAYS IT GIVES UP
 *
 *   - prefers-reduced-motion: ONE frame is drawn at a fixed phase and the loop never starts. Not a
 *     blank box — the grid, the band, the wash and the whole sky are all there, they simply do not
 *     move. The still frame is a finished composition, which is the only version of "less motion"
 *     that is not just "less".
 *   - a hidden tab: the loop is cancelled on visibilitychange and restarted on return. A background
 *     tab burning a core is the usual price of an effect like this and it is entirely avoidable.
 *   - no 2D context: nothing is drawn, the canvas stays transparent, and whatever is behind it shows
 *     through untouched.
 *
 * ================================ TWO MECHANICAL THINGS THAT COST THIS REPOSITORY A DEPLOY
 *
 *   - `document.querySelector`, never `document.currentScript`. Astro hoists a component's <script>
 *     into a bundled `<script type="module">`, and `currentScript` is null in every module script by
 *     spec. The hero effect that shipped this morning found its canvas that way, resolved to
 *     undefined, and never ran — while the <canvas>, the class and the bundle were all present in
 *     the served HTML. `tests/horizon-runs.test.mjs` executes this script and counts what comes out
 *     of the context, because that is the only reading that can tell a live canvas from a dead one.
 *   - devicePixelRatio is capped at 2. A 3x phone otherwise pays for nine times the pixels to draw
 *     lines nobody can resolve.
 *
 * It is decoration. It carries aria-hidden, it takes no pointer events, and a screen reader is told
 * nothing about it.
 */
---
<canvas class="horizon" aria-hidden="true"></canvas>

<style>
  /* Fixed and full-viewport at z-index -1, which is the reference's own structure. It works here
     for a specific reason worth writing down: `html` declares no background, so `body`'s --ground
     propagates to the viewport canvas and is painted BENEATH the negative-z layer rather than over
     it. A background moved onto `html` later would hide this element completely. */
  .horizon {
    position: fixed;
    inset: 0;
    width: 100%;
    height: 100%;
    z-index: -1;
    display: block;
    pointer-events: none;
    /* The sky is held back from the top of the frame so the nav row sits on the page's own colour
       and not on drifting dust. The floor is not masked: its own falloff is drawn. */
    mask-image: linear-gradient(180deg, transparent 0%, #000 24%);
    -webkit-mask-image: linear-gradient(180deg, transparent 0%, #000 24%);
  }

  /* The still frame is the same composition, quieter. A motionless field at full strength reads as
     a texture somebody forgot to finish. */
  @media (prefers-reduced-motion: reduce) {
    .horizon { opacity: 0.58; }
  }
</style>

<script>
  // NOT `document.currentScript` — see the note at the top of this file. That spelling is null in a
  // module script, and Astro makes every component script a module script.
  const canvas = document.querySelector('canvas.horizon');

  if (canvas instanceof HTMLCanvasElement) {
    const ctx = canvas.getContext('2d');

    // No 2D context: draw nothing at all and leave the element transparent, so whatever sits behind
    // it is exactly what a visitor sees.
    if (ctx) {
      const reduced = window.matchMedia('(prefers-reduced-motion: reduce)');
      const TAU = Math.PI * 2;

      /* ---------------------------------------------------------------- the theme, read not typed */

      /*
       * EVERY FUNCTION BELOW IS A `const` ARROW, AND THAT IS NOT A STYLE PREFERENCE.
       *
       * `canvas` and `ctx` are narrowed once, at the top, by `instanceof` and by a truthiness
       * check. TypeScript keeps the narrowing of a `const` inside a closure that is CREATED after
       * it — but a `function` declaration is hoisted, so it could in principle run before the
       * check, and TS drops the narrowing for the whole body. Written as declarations this file
       * produced 45 `'ctx' is possibly null` and `Property 'width' does not exist on type
       * 'Element'` errors under `astro check`, which is exactly the count its sibling FlowField.astro
       * still carries. Nothing about the running code changes; the checker simply stops being blind
       * to the guard that is already there.
       *
       * The parameter defaults are the same trick without TypeScript syntax: they give the checker
       * a type to infer under `strict` while the file stays plain JavaScript, which matters because
       * `tests/horizon-runs.test.mjs` executes this script text directly in a `vm`.
       */
      const token = (name = '', fallback = '') => {
        const value = getComputedStyle(document.documentElement).getPropertyValue(name).trim();
        return value || fallback;
      };

      /** '#00d492' | '#0a9' | 'rgb(0, 212, 146)' -> [r, g, b]. */
      const channels = (value = '', fallback = [0, 0, 0]) => {
        const hex = /^#([0-9a-f]{3}|[0-9a-f]{6})$/i.exec(value);
        if (hex) {
          const raw = hex[1].length === 3 ? hex[1].replace(/./g, (c) => c + c) : hex[1];
          return [0, 2, 4].map((at) => parseInt(raw.slice(at, at + 2), 16));
        }
        const fn = /rgba?\(\s*([\d.]+)[\s,]+([\d.]+)[\s,]+([\d.]+)/i.exec(value);
        if (fn) return [Number(fn[1]), Number(fn[2]), Number(fn[3])];
        return fallback;
      };

      // Read off the document rather than hardcoded, so one token change cannot leave one green on
      // the page and a different green behind it — and so the light ramp gets a light horizon.
      let accent = [0, 212, 146];
      let ground = [5, 8, 7];
      const readTheme = () => {
        accent = channels(token('--accent', '#00d492'), [0, 212, 146]);
        ground = channels(token('--ground', '#050807'), [5, 8, 7]);
      };
      const rgba = (c = [0, 0, 0], a = 1) => `rgba(${c[0]},${c[1]},${c[2]},${a})`;

      /* ------------------------------------------------------------------------------ the numbers */

      const HORIZON = 0.74;      // the horizon line, as a fraction of the viewport height
      const RUNGS = 26;          // ground cross-lines between the viewer and the vanishing point
      const RAILS = 15;          // converging rails either side of centre
      const SKY = 110;           // drifting particles above the horizon
      const TRAVEL = 0.000115;   // phase per millisecond: one rung passes the viewer every ~8.7s
      const EASE = 0.055;        // how hard the vanishing point chases the cursor, per frame

      let w = 0;
      let h = 0;
      let dpr = 1;
      let vpx = 0;               // the eased vanishing point
      let vpy = 0;
      let themeReadAt = -1e9;

      const pointer = { x: -9999, y: -9999, on: false };

      /** One mote of the drifting sky. */
      const mote = () => ({
        x: Math.random() * w,
        y: Math.random() * h * HORIZON,
        z: 0.25 + Math.random() * 0.75,        // parallax depth
        r: 0.5 + Math.random() * 1.2,
        a: 0.1 + Math.random() * 0.34,
        phase: Math.random() * TAU,
      });

      // TYPED BY ITS FIRST MEMBER RATHER THAN BY AN ANNOTATION, so this file stays plain JavaScript
      // and `tests/horizon-runs.test.mjs` can execute its text in a `vm` without a compile step. A
      // bare `[]` is an evolving `any[]`, and `strict` cannot resolve one that is filled in one
      // closure and read in another. The sky is REPLACED on every seed, never emptied in place.
      let dust = [mote()];

      const resize = () => {
        dpr = Math.min(window.devicePixelRatio || 1, 2);
        const r = canvas.getBoundingClientRect();
        w = Math.max(1, Math.round(r.width));
        h = Math.max(1, Math.round(r.height));
        canvas.width = Math.round(w * dpr);
        canvas.height = Math.round(h * dpr);
        ctx.setTransform(dpr, 0, 0, dpr, 0, 0);
        vpx = w / 2;
        vpy = h * HORIZON;
      };

      const seed = () => { dust = Array.from({ length: SKY }, mote); };

      /* -------------------------------------------------------------------------------- the frame */

      const draw = (t = 0) => {
        // A horizon is not a flow field: it is cleared every frame. A wash here would smear the
        // grid into a fog, which is the same mistake as clearing a flow field.
        ctx.clearRect(0, 0, w, h);
        ctx.globalAlpha = 1;

        if (t - themeReadAt > 500) { readTheme(); themeReadAt = t; }

        // THE POINTER MOVES THE VANISHING POINT. This is the whole reason the layer is a canvas
        // rather than a fixed image: an image cannot lean. x is given real range; y is held to a
        // narrow band, because a horizon that slides up and down the screen stops being a horizon.
        const base = h * HORIZON;
        const wantX = pointer.on ? w / 2 + (pointer.x - w / 2) * 0.24 : w / 2;
        const wantY = pointer.on
          ? Math.max(base - h * 0.03, Math.min(base + h * 0.03, base + (pointer.y - base) * 0.05))
          : base;
        vpx += (wantX - vpx) * EASE;
        vpy += (wantY - vpy) * EASE;

        const floor = Math.max(1, h - vpy);
        const lean = (vpx - w / 2) / Math.max(1, w);

        /* 1. THE GLOW BAND ------------------------------------------------------------------- */
        const top = Math.max(0, vpy - 92);
        const bottom = Math.min(h, vpy + 150);
        if (bottom - top > 2) {
          const band = ctx.createLinearGradient(0, top, 0, bottom);
          const peak = Math.min(0.999, Math.max(0.001, (vpy - top) / (bottom - top)));
          band.addColorStop(0, rgba(accent, 0));
          band.addColorStop(peak * 0.55, rgba(accent, 0.055));
          band.addColorStop(peak, rgba(accent, 0.24));
          band.addColorStop(Math.min(1, peak + (1 - peak) * 0.38), rgba(accent, 0.07));
          band.addColorStop(1, rgba(accent, 0));
          ctx.fillStyle = band;
          ctx.fillRect(0, top, w, bottom - top);
        }
        // The core. Without it the band is a smudge rather than a horizon.
        ctx.fillStyle = rgba(accent, 0.52);
        ctx.fillRect(0, vpy - 0.6, w, 1.2);

        /* 2. THE FLOOR ----------------------------------------------------------------------- */
        ctx.strokeStyle = rgba(accent, 1);
        ctx.lineWidth = 1;

        // Rails: equally spaced along the bottom edge, every one drawn to the vanishing point.
        const spread = w * 1.9;
        const step = spread / RAILS;
        for (let i = -RAILS; i <= RAILS; i++) {
          const edge = Math.min(1, Math.abs(i) / RAILS);
          ctx.globalAlpha = 0.2 * (1 - edge * 0.74);
          ctx.beginPath();
          ctx.moveTo(vpx, vpy);
          ctx.lineTo(vpx + i * step, h);
          ctx.stroke();
        }

        // Rungs: the ground's cross-lines, scrolling toward the reader.
        const phase = (t * TRAVEL) % 1;
        for (let n = 1; n <= RUNGS; n++) {
          const depth = n - phase;
          if (depth <= 0.02) continue;
          const y = vpy + floor / depth;
          if (y > h + 2) continue;
          const u = (y - vpy) / floor;           // 0 at the horizon, 1 at the bottom edge
          ctx.globalAlpha = 0.28 * Math.pow(u, 0.5) * (1 - u * 0.3);
          ctx.beginPath();
          ctx.moveTo(0, y);
          ctx.lineTo(w, y);
          ctx.stroke();
        }
        ctx.globalAlpha = 1;

        /* 3. THE BOTTOM WASH, in the page's own ground colour -------------------------------- */
        const washTop = vpy + floor * 0.72;
        if (h - washTop > 2) {
          const wash = ctx.createLinearGradient(0, washTop, 0, h);
          wash.addColorStop(0, rgba(ground, 0));
          wash.addColorStop(1, rgba(ground, 0.88));
          ctx.fillStyle = wash;
          ctx.fillRect(0, washTop, w, h - washTop);
        }

        /* 4. THE SKY ------------------------------------------------------------------------- */
        ctx.fillStyle = rgba(accent, 1);
        for (const p of dust) {
          p.x += p.z * 0.16;
          p.y -= p.z * 0.055;
          if (p.x > w + 6) p.x = -6;
          if (p.x < -6) p.x = w + 6;
          // Off the top, or somehow below the horizon: born again on the band it is lit by.
          if (p.y < -6 || p.y > vpy) {
            p.y = vpy - Math.random() * 6;
            p.x = Math.random() * w;
          }
          const sway = Math.sin(t * 0.00042 + p.phase) * 7 * p.z;
          // A HUMP, NOT A RAMP, and the first version got this backwards in both directions.
          // Ramping up toward the horizon puts the dust inside the glow, where it cannot be seen;
          // ramping the other way puts it under the CSS mask, where it also cannot be seen. Peak in
          // the middle of the sky is the one band where a 1px dot on a near-black page reads.
          const lit = Math.sin(Math.PI * Math.min(1, Math.max(0, p.y) / Math.max(1, vpy)));
          ctx.globalAlpha = p.a * (0.18 + 0.82 * lit);
          ctx.beginPath();
          ctx.arc(p.x + sway + lean * 120 * p.z, p.y, p.r, 0, TAU);
          ctx.fill();
        }
        ctx.globalAlpha = 1;
      };

      /* ------------------------------------------------------------------------- the loop, and off */

      let raf = 0;
      const loop = (t = 0) => { draw(t); raf = requestAnimationFrame(loop); };
      const start = () => { if (!raf && !reduced.matches) raf = requestAnimationFrame(loop); };
      const stop = () => { if (raf) { cancelAnimationFrame(raf); raf = 0; } };

      const boot = () => {
        resize();
        readTheme();
        seed();
        if (reduced.matches) {
          // ONE still frame, at a phase chosen so the rungs are mid-stride rather than stacked on
          // the edges of the floor. Everything is present; nothing moves again.
          themeReadAt = -1e9;
          draw(4200);
          stop();
        } else {
          start();
        }
      };

      addEventListener('resize', () => {
        const running = !!raf;
        stop();
        boot();
        if (!running) stop();
      }, { passive: true });

      addEventListener('pointermove', (e) => {
        const r = canvas.getBoundingClientRect();
        pointer.x = e.clientX - r.left;
        pointer.y = e.clientY - r.top;
        pointer.on = true;
      }, { passive: true });

      addEventListener('pointerleave', () => { pointer.on = false; }, { passive: true });

      // A backgrounded tab must not burn a core.
      document.addEventListener('visibilitychange', () => (document.hidden ? stop() : start()));

      reduced.addEventListener?.('change', () => { stop(); boot(); });

      boot();
    }
  }
</script>
